 <exercise xml:id="exercise-eigen-definition">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Verify that
      <m>
        \mathbf{v} =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
      </m>
      is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>, and find its corresponding eigenvalue.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}</m> and check whether it is a scalar multiple
      of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>\lambda=3</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We compute
      <m>
        A\mathbf{v}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \\
          1
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3 \\
          3
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since
      <m>
        A\mathbf{v} = 3\mathbf{v},
      </m>
      the vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is an eigenvector and the corresponding
      eigenvalue is <m>\lambda=3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-find-eigenvalues-2x2">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the eigenvalues of the matrix
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp -2 \\
          1 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Eigenvalues satisfy <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda=2</m> and <m>\lambda=3</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We compute
      <m>
        \det(A-\lambda I)
        =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4-\lambda \amp -2 \\
          1 \amp 1-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        (4-\lambda)(1-\lambda)+2.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Expanding,
      <m>
        (4-\lambda)(1-\lambda)+2
        = \lambda^2 -5\lambda +6.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Setting this equal to zero,
      <m>
        \lambda^2 -5\lambda +6 = 0,
      </m>
      which factors as
      <m>
        (\lambda-2)(\lambda-3)=0.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda=2</m> and <m>\lambda=3</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-eigen-geometry-enhanced">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp -1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <ol> <li><p>
      Describe geometrically how <m>A</m> acts on vectors in
      <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>. </p> </li>
    <li> <p> Identify all eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and describe the
      corresponding eigenvectors geometrically.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Examine what happens to vectors lying on each coordinate axis,
      and interpret the sign and magnitude of the resulting scalar multiples.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The matrix stretches vectors on the <m>x</m>-axis by a factor of 2
      and reflects vectors on the <m>y</m>-axis across the origin.
      The eigenvalues are <m>2</m> and <m>-1</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <ol> <li> <p>
      The matrix <m>A</m> acts independently on each coordinate.
      A vector
      <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}x\\y\end{bmatrix}
      </m>
      is sent to
      <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}2x\\-y\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Geometrically, this stretches space horizontally by a factor of 2
      and reflects vectors vertically across the origin.
    </p>
  </li>
   <li> <p>
      For vectors on the <m>x</m>-axis, we have
      <m>
        A\begin{bmatrix}x\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}2x\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        2\begin{bmatrix}x\\0\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Every nonzero vector on the <m>x</m>-axis is therefore an eigenvector
      with eigenvalue <m>2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For vectors on the <m>y</m>-axis,
      <m>
        A\begin{bmatrix}0\\y\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\-y\end{bmatrix}
        =
        -1\begin{bmatrix}0\\y\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      Every nonzero vector on the <m>y</m>-axis is an eigenvector
      with eigenvalue <m>-1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus, <m>A</m> has two distinct eigenvalues with eigendirections
      aligned with the coordinate axes, one producing stretching and
      the other producing a reflection.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-eigenvectors-linear-combination">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the matrix
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix},
      </m>
      and the vectors
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}_1 =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        \mathbf{v}_2 =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        \mathbf{v}_3 =
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
<ol> <li>
    <p>
      Show that <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>, and
      <m>\mathbf{v}_3</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>, and find their
      associated eigenvalues.
    </p>
  </li>
<li>
    <p>
      Express the vector
      <m>
        \mathbf{x} =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3\\-4\\2
        \end{bmatrix}
      </m>
      as a linear combination of the eigenvectors.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Use this expression to compute <m>A\mathbf{x}</m>,
      <m>A^2\mathbf{x}</m>, and <m>A^{-1}\mathbf{x}</m> as linear
      combinations of the eigenvectors.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      For part (a), check whether <m>A\mathbf{v}_i</m> is a scalar multiple
      of <m>\mathbf{v}_i</m>.  
      For parts (b) and (c), work entirely in the eigenvector basis.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>4</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>1</m>.
      The vector <m>\mathbf{x}</m> can be written as
      <m>
        \mathbf{x}
        = \mathbf{v}_1 + 2\mathbf{v}_2 + 2\mathbf{v}_3.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
<ol>
  <li>
    <p>
      We compute:
      <me>
        A\mathbf{v}_1
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}4\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        4\mathbf{v}_1,
      </me>
      so <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <m>4</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Next,
      <me>
        A\mathbf{v}_2
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\-4\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        2\mathbf{v}_2,
      </me>
      so <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> has eigenvalue <m>2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally,
      <me>
        A\mathbf{v}_3
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \mathbf{v}_3,
      </me>
      so the eigenvalue is <m>1</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
<li>
    <p>
      We seek scalars <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> such that
      <me>
        a\mathbf{v}_1 + b\mathbf{v}_2 + c\mathbf{v}_3
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}3\\-4\\2\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      This gives
      <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
          a + b \\
          -2b \\
          c
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}3\\-4\\2\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      Solving, we find <m>b=2</m>, <m>a=1</m>, and <m>c=2</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
<li>
   <p>
  Since each eigenvector satisfies
  <m>A\mathbf{v}_i = \lambda_i\mathbf{v}_i</m>, we can apply powers and
  inverses of <m>A</m> by scaling each eigenvector.
  Using
  <m>
    \mathbf{x}
    = \mathbf{v}_1 + 2\mathbf{v}_2 + 2\mathbf{v}_3,
  </m>
  we compute:
</p>

<p>
  <md>
    <mrow> A\mathbf{x}
    \amp = 1(4\mathbf{v}_1) + 2(2\mathbf{v}_2) + 2(1\mathbf{v}_3) </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = 4\mathbf{v}_1 + 4\mathbf{v}_2 + 2\mathbf{v}_3. </mrow>
  </md>
</p>

<p>
  Similarly,
  <md>
    <mrow> A^2\mathbf{x}
    \amp = 1(4^2\mathbf{v}_1) + 2(2^2\mathbf{v}_2) + 2(1^2\mathbf{v}_3) </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = 16\mathbf{v}_1 + 8\mathbf{v}_2 + 2\mathbf{v}_3. </mrow>
  </md>
</p>

<p>
  Since
  <m>A^{-1}\mathbf{v}_i = \tfrac{1}{\lambda_i}\mathbf{v}_i</m>, we obtain
</p>

<p>
  <md>
    <mrow> A^{-1}\mathbf{x}
    \amp = 1\left(\tfrac{1}{4}\mathbf{v}_1\right)
      + 2\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{v}_2\right)
      + 2(1\mathbf{v}_3) </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = \tfrac{1}{4}\mathbf{v}_1 + \mathbf{v}_2 + 2\mathbf{v}_3. </mrow>
  </md>
</p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-eigenvectors-given-lambda">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Find a basis for the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue
      <m>\lambda=2</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Solve <m>(A-2I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      A basis is
      <m>
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}-1\\2\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We form
      <m>
        A-2I =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      The equation <m>(A-2I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m> becomes
      <m>2x_1+x_2=0</m>.
      Solving gives <m>x_2=-2x_1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus eigenvectors are multiples of
      <m>\begin{bmatrix}-1\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>,
      which forms a basis for the eigenspace.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-eigenvalues-triangular">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Compute the eigenvalues of
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 5
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Consider how the determinant of <m>A-\lambda I</m> simplifies.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>1</m>, <m>3</m>, and <m>5</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>A-\lambda I</m> is upper triangular,
      its determinant is the product of the diagonal entries:
      <m>
        (1-\lambda)(3-\lambda)(5-\lambda).
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Setting this equal to zero gives the eigenvalues
      <m>\lambda=1</m>, <m>\lambda=3</m>, and <m>\lambda=5</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-eigenvalue-geometry">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose a <m>2\times2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has a single eigenvalue
      <m>\lambda</m> of multiplicity two.
    </p>
    <p>
      Explain what this means for the action of <m>A</m> on vectors in
      <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Consider how many independent directions are preserved by the transformation.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      All eigenvectors lie along a single line through the origin.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      A repeated eigenvalue means that the characteristic polynomial
      has only one root.
    </p>
    <p>
      The eigenspace may still be one‑dimensional, meaning there is
      only one line of vectors that are stretched by the transformation.
      Vectors not on this line are generally rotated or sheared.
    </p>
    <p>
      This situation contrasts with matrices that have two distinct
      eigenvalues, which preserve two independent directions.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, consider the matrix
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 3
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      This matrix has a single eigenvalue of multiplicity two.
      We analyze its eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and geometric behavior.
    </p>

    <p>
      We compute the characteristic polynomial:
      <m>
        \det(A-\lambda I)
        =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3-\lambda \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 3-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        (3-\lambda)^2.
      </m>
      Thus, <m>\lambda=3</m> is the only eigenvalue, with multiplicity two.
    </p>

    <p>
      To find the eigenvectors, we solve
      <m>(A-3I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>:
      <m>
        A-3I =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          0 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <p>
      This gives the equation <m>x_2=0</m>, so eigenvectors have the form
      <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}x\\0\end{bmatrix}, \; x\neq 0.
      </m>
      The eigenspace is therefore one‑dimensional, consisting of all
      nonzero vectors along the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>

    <p>
      Geometrically, the transformation scales vectors by a factor of 3,
      but also shears vectors that are not on the eigenline.
      Only vectors lying on the <m>x</m>-axis preserve their direction.
    </p>

    <p>
      This example illustrates that a repeated eigenvalue does not
      guarantee two independent eigenvectors. Even though the eigenvalue
      has multiplicity two, there is only one invariant direction in
      <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalizable-or-not">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Determine whether <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
      Justify your answer.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Find the eigenvalues and the dimension of each eigenspace.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The characteristic polynomial of <m>A</m> is
      <m>
        \det(A-\lambda I)
        =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2-\lambda \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 2-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix}
        = (2-\lambda)^2.
      </m>
      Thus the only eigenvalue is <m>\lambda=2</m>, with algebraic
      multiplicity 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find the eigenspace, we solve
      <m>(A-2I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>:
      <m>
        A-2I =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          0 \amp 1 \\
          0 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      This gives <m>x_2=0</m>, so the eigenspace is one‑dimensional.
      Because there are fewer eigenvectors than the size of the matrix,
      <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-3x3-repeated-clean">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the matrix
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 3
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <ol> <li>
    <p>
      Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and their algebraic multiplicities. </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Find a basis for each eigenspace.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li> <p>
     Diagonalize <m>A</m> by finding matrices <m>P</m> and <m>D</m>
      such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      First compute <m>\det(A-\lambda I)</m>.
      Then solve <m>(A-\lambda I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m> for each eigenvalue.
      Remember that <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if it has three linearly
      independent eigenvectors.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>3</m> with multiplicity 2 and <m>1</m> with
      multiplicity 1. The matrix is diagonalizable.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <ol> <li>
    <p>
      We compute
      <md>
        <mrow> \det(A-\lambda I)
        \amp =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2-\lambda \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          1 \amp 2-\lambda \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 3-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =
        (3-\lambda)\big((2-\lambda)^2-1\big). </mrow>
      </md>
      This factors as
      <m>(3-\lambda)(\lambda-1)(\lambda-3)</m>.
      Thus the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda=3</m> (multiplicity 2) and
      <m>\lambda=1</m> (multiplicity 1).
    </p>
  </li> 
  <li>
    <p>
      For <m>\lambda=3</m>, we solve
      <m>(A-3I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>, which gives
      <m>x_1=x_2</m> with <m>x_3</m> free.
      A basis for this eigenspace is
      <me>
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda=1</m>, solving <m>(A-I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>
      yields <m>x_1=-x_2</m> and <m>x_3=0</m>.
      A basis for this eigenspace is
      <me>
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Since there are three linearly independent eigenvectors,
      <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
      Let
      <me>
        P  =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp 0
        \end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        D =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      Then <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-diagonalization-2x2-nth-power">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3 \amp 2 \\
          2 \amp 3
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <ol> <li>
    <p>
      Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m>. </p>
    </li>
    <li> <p>
      Diagonalize <m>A</m> by finding matrices <m>P</m> and <m>D</m>
      such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>. </p> 
    </li>
    <li> <p>
      Use the diagonalization to find a formula for <m>A^n</m>,
      where <m>n</m> is a positive integer.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues satisfy <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m>.
      If <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, then <m>A^n = PD^nP^{-1}</m>.
      Powers of a diagonal matrix are easy to compute.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>5</m> and <m>1</m>.
      A formula for <m>A^n</m> is
      <me>
        A^n
        =
        \frac{1}{2}
        \begin{bmatrix}
          5^n + 1^n \amp 5^n - 1^n \\
          5^n - 1^n \amp 5^n + 1^n
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <ol> <li>
    <p>
      We compute the characteristic polynomial:
      <md>
        <mrow> \det(A-\lambda I)
        \amp =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3-\lambda \amp 2 \\
          2 \amp 3-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =
        (3-\lambda)^2 - 4 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \lambda^2 - 6\lambda + 5.</mrow>
      </md>
      Factoring gives
      <m>(\lambda-5)(\lambda-1)=0</m>.
      Thus the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda=5</m> and <m>\lambda=1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>\lambda=5</m>, solving <m>(A-5I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>
      yields eigenvectors proportional to
      <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      For <m>\lambda=1</m>, solving <m>(A-I)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}</m>
      yields eigenvectors proportional to
      <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Let
      <me>
        P =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp -1
        \end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        D =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          5 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      Since the columns of <m>P</m> are linearly independent,
      <m>P</m> is invertible and <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Using <m>A^n = PD^nP^{-1}</m>, we compute
      <me>
        D^n =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          5^n \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp 1^n
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      Carrying out the multiplication gives
      <me>
        A^n
        =
        \frac{1}{2}
        \begin{bmatrix}
          5^n + 1^n \amp 5^n - 1^n \\
          5^n - 1^n \amp 5^n + 1^n
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-complex-eigenvalues-real-diagonalization">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 2 \\
          -3 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
      This matrix has complex eigenvalues. In this exercise, we show how
      to construct a real matrix <m>P</m> such that
      <m>A\</m> is similar over the reals to a matrix of the form
      <m>
        C =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          a \amp -b \\
          b \amp a
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        Show that the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are complex.
      </li>

      <li>
        Choose one eigenvalue <m>\lambda = a + bi</m> and write down the
        corresponding matrix <m>C</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Using the same eigenvalue, find a complex eigenvector
        <m>\mathbf v</m> by solving
        <m>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf v = \mathbf 0</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        Write <m>\mathbf v = \mathbf v_1 + i\mathbf v_2</m>, where
        <m>\mathbf v_1</m> and <m>\mathbf v_2</m> have real entries.
      </li>

      <li>
        Let <m>P = [\mathbf v_1\ \mathbf v_2]</m>.
        Verify directly that
        <m>A\mathbf v_1 = a\mathbf v_1 - b\mathbf v_2</m> and
        <m>A\mathbf v_2 = b\mathbf v_1 + a\mathbf v_2</m>,
        and conclude that <m>A = PCP^{-1}</m>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      For part (a), compute <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m>.
      For the final part, do not compute <m>P^{-1}</m> directly; instead,
      use how <m>A</m> acts on <m>\mathbf v_1</m> and <m>\mathbf v_2</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The eigenvalues are <m>1 \pm i\sqrt{6}</m>.
      One valid choice of <m>P</m> satisfies <m>A = PCP^{-1}</m> with
      <me>
        C =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp -\sqrt{6} \\
          \sqrt{6} \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <ol> <li>
    <p>
      We compute
      <md>
        <mrow> \det(A - \lambda I)
        \amp =
        \det
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1-\lambda \amp 2 \\
          -3 \amp 1-\lambda
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =
        (1-\lambda)^2 + 6. </mrow>
      </md>
      Setting this equal to zero gives
      <m>\lambda = 1 \pm i\sqrt{6}</m>, so the eigenvalues are complex.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Choosing <m>\lambda = 1 + i\sqrt{6}</m>, we have
      <m>a = 1</m> and <m>b = \sqrt{6}</m>, and therefore
      <me>
        C =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp -\sqrt{6} \\
          \sqrt{6} \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Solving <m>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf v = \mathbf 0</m> with
      <m>\lambda = 1 + i\sqrt{6}</m> yields the eigenvector
      <me>
        \mathbf v =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \\
          i\sqrt{6}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Writing <m>\mathbf v = \mathbf v_1 + i\mathbf v_2</m>, we obtain
      <me>
        \mathbf v_1 =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \\
          0
        \end{bmatrix},
        \qquad
        \mathbf v_2 =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          0 \\
          \sqrt{6}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Let
      <me>
        P =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 0 \\
          0 \amp \sqrt{6}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
      We compute
      <md>
        <mrow> A\mathbf v_1 =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \\
          -6
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \mathbf v_1 - \sqrt{6}\mathbf v_2, </mrow>
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        <mrow> A\mathbf v_2 =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2\sqrt{6} \\
          \sqrt{6}
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \sqrt{6}\mathbf v_1 + \mathbf v_2. </mrow>
      </md>
      These identities show that
      <m>A[\mathbf v_1\ \mathbf v_2] = [\mathbf v_1\ \mathbf v_2]C</m>,
      and since <m>P</m> is invertible, we conclude that
      <m>A = PCP^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
  </solution>
</exercise>


</exercises>

</section>
</handout>